\paragraph{(k) Aggregation penalty.} Per-test classification achieves 84.48\% per-test accuracy at repo level (Qwen3-8B, seed 42), but variant-level accuracy under the all-pass rule drops to 72.92\% (35/48 variants). The all-pass rule requires \emph{every} test in a variant to be correctly predicted; a single false negative flips the variant verdict. For variants with hundreds of tests (e.g., \texttt{astropy\_\_astropy-7336}: 357 tests, 2 false negatives $\to$ incorrect variant), even 99.4\% per-test accuracy produces a wrong variant-level prediction. This aggregation penalty is predictable from test count: function-level variants ($\bar{T} \approx 2.3$) incur negligible penalty (per-test $\approx$ variant-level), while repo-level variants ($\bar{T} \gg 10$) incur substantial penalty. The finding connects to the oracle analysis (\S\ref{sec:analysis}(a)): per-test granularity concentrates its \emph{advantage} at $T \geq 2$ but also its \emph{cost} at high $T$.

